{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"Authorized participants (APs) can create and redeem ETF shares directly with the fund, usually in large blocks (“creation units”), exchanging a basket of securities or cash. This mechanism helps align an ETF’s market price with its net asset value.\n\nMarket makers quote bids and offers and trade ETF shares on exchanges, providing liquidity to investors. They may be APs, but need not be. When useful, a market maker can hedge or arbitrage a price mismatch—and may use an AP’s creation/redemption process—but ordinary exchange trading does not require AP status.\n\nIn short: **AP** describes a role in the primary market; **market maker** describes a liquidity-providing role in the secondary market. One firm can perform both."}}
